// ListDeployEnvironmentConnectionsWithResponse The connection aliases of a deploy environment, and what each is bound to
//
// THE DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT'S CONNECTIONS TAB: "Every ALIAS used by deployed flows, each bound to
// a concrete Integrations connection."
//
// AN ALIAS IS WHAT A STEP NAMES WHEN ITS CONNECTION "DIFFERS PER ENVIRONMENT": "the step then
// references an ALIAS which each environment binds to a concrete connection". The flow's file
// carries the alias and never the connection, so one flow runs on Development's connection in
// Development and on Production's in Production.
//
// TWO KINDS OF ROW, IN ONE LIST. An alias a flow live here uses, bound or not, and an alias
// bound here that no live flow uses yet: a binding is made before the deploy that needs it,
// because "Unbound alias = blocking deploy-validation error". `connection_id` is `null` on an
// alias nothing is bound to, and `live_flow_count` is 0 on one no live flow uses.
//
// THE BINDING IS FLOWS', AND ONLY HERE: per deploy environment, never in the flow's file and
// never in Integrations (the spec lane, 28 October 2026, ONPLO-3655). Which deploy
// environments a connection may be used in at all is Integrations'
// (`getConnectionDeployEnvironments`), and is not this.
//
// IN ALIAS ORDER, a page at a time. AN ARCHIVED DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT IS READ like any other.
//
// `flow_ids` NAMES ONLY FLOWS YOU MAY SEE (decision 0941). Seeing a flow can be given on one flow
// alone, and reading the deploy environment says nothing about a flow. So Flows takes the first
// 200 flows using the alias, in id order, and every one of those is asked `flows:flow.read` once
// the page is read. One you may not see is left out of the list, and the row stays.
//
// SO AN EMPTY `flow_ids` DOES NOT MEAN NO FLOW USES THE ALIAS. It means that of the first 200, you
// may see none. `live_flow_count` says how many use it, whatever was cut or left out: unbinding an
// alias a flow still uses blocks that flow's next deploy.
//
// THE FLOWS ARE ASKED AFTER THE PAGE IS READ. If the asking fails, the whole answer does: nothing
// is shown, and sending the call again is safe.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /deploy-environments/{deploy_environment_id}/connections (the `ListDeployEnvironmentConnections` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListDeployEnvironmentConnectionsWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deployEnvironmentId DeployEnvironmentId, params *ListDeployEnvironmentConnectionsParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListDeployEnvironmentConnectionsResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListDeployEnvironmentConnections(ctx, deployEnvironmentId, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListDeployEnvironmentConnectionsResponse(rsp)
}

// UnbindDeployEnvironmentConnectionWithResponse Unbind an alias in a deploy environment
//
// THE ALIAS IS BOUND TO NOTHING HERE, AT ONCE. "Unbind an alias from a deploy environment; a
// deployed flow that still uses it is named first."
//
// THE SCREEN NAMES THEM FIRST, AND AN API HAS NO SCREEN: read the alias's `live_flow_count` and
// `flow_ids` with `listDeployEnvironmentConnections`. `flow_ids` holds only flows you may see, so
// the count may be higher. Unbinding does not wait on them. A flow live here
// that still uses the alias is not taken off; its next deploy is blocked with
// `unbound_alias` until the alias is bound again.
//
// THE CONNECTION NO LONGER NAMES THOSE FLOWS. Each use this binding registered with
// Integrations is released, unless the flow still uses the same connection another way.
//
// AN ALIAS BOUND TO NOTHING HERE IS NOT AN ERROR: nothing left to unbind is a complete
// unbind. AN ARCHIVED DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT IS REFUSED with `deploy_environment_archived`, as a
// bind is.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with DELETE /deploy-environments/{deploy_environment_id}/connections/{alias} (the `UnbindDeployEnvironmentConnection` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) UnbindDeployEnvironmentConnectionWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deployEnvironmentId DeployEnvironmentId, alias ConnectionAliasParameter, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*UnbindDeployEnvironmentConnectionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.UnbindDeployEnvironmentConnection(ctx, deployEnvironmentId, alias, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseUnbindDeployEnvironmentConnectionResponse(rsp)
}

// BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionWithBodyWithResponse Bind an alias to a connection in a deploy environment
//
// A STEP THAT NAMES THIS ALIAS USES THIS CONNECTION, IN THIS DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT. "Binding an
// alias to an Integrations connection on the Connections tab is an act of its own."
//
// BINDING AN ALIAS ALREADY BOUND REPLACES ITS CONNECTION, and sending the connection it has
// changes nothing: the same request sent twice is the same act. A flow live here takes the new
// connection at its next step that names the alias; a step already running finishes on the
// one it started with.
//
// THE CONNECTION MUST BE ONE YOU MAY USE, of this account environment. One that does not
// exist for you, one in another account environment and one you may not use are refused the
// same way: 422, naming `connection_id`.
//
// IT TAKES TWO PERMISSIONS. Binding is an act on this deploy environment AND pointing
// something at a connection, which is a permission of the Integrations app:
// `integrations:connection.assign`. "Integrations' own right to use that connection is asked
// too." Your credential must carry both, and a refusal is one 403 whose detail names both.
//
// THE CONNECTION THEN SAYS SO. Each flow live here that uses the alias is registered with
// Integrations as a use of the connection, so the connection's "used by" list names the flow.
// A connection replaced is released for each of them, unless the flow still uses it another way.
//
// THE ANSWER COUNTS THOSE FLOWS AND NAMES NONE. Binding already asks a second permission, so the
// door cannot also ask about each flow. `listDeployEnvironmentConnections` names the ones you
// may see.
//
// AN ARCHIVED DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT IS REFUSED with `deploy_environment_archived`: it takes no
// new runs, so nothing would ever use the binding.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PUT /deploy-environments/{deploy_environment_id}/connections/{alias} (the `BindDeployEnvironmentConnection` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deployEnvironmentId DeployEnvironmentId, alias ConnectionAliasParameter, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionWithBody(ctx, deployEnvironmentId, alias, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseBindDeployEnvironmentConnectionResponse(rsp)
}

// BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionWithResponse Bind an alias to a connection in a deploy environment
//
// A STEP THAT NAMES THIS ALIAS USES THIS CONNECTION, IN THIS DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT. "Binding an
// alias to an Integrations connection on the Connections tab is an act of its own."
//
// BINDING AN ALIAS ALREADY BOUND REPLACES ITS CONNECTION, and sending the connection it has
// changes nothing: the same request sent twice is the same act. A flow live here takes the new
// connection at its next step that names the alias; a step already running finishes on the
// one it started with.
//
// THE CONNECTION MUST BE ONE YOU MAY USE, of this account environment. One that does not
// exist for you, one in another account environment and one you may not use are refused the
// same way: 422, naming `connection_id`.
//
// IT TAKES TWO PERMISSIONS. Binding is an act on this deploy environment AND pointing
// something at a connection, which is a permission of the Integrations app:
// `integrations:connection.assign`. "Integrations' own right to use that connection is asked
// too." Your credential must carry both, and a refusal is one 403 whose detail names both.
//
// THE CONNECTION THEN SAYS SO. Each flow live here that uses the alias is registered with
// Integrations as a use of the connection, so the connection's "used by" list names the flow.
// A connection replaced is released for each of them, unless the flow still uses it another way.
//
// THE ANSWER COUNTS THOSE FLOWS AND NAMES NONE. Binding already asks a second permission, so the
// door cannot also ask about each flow. `listDeployEnvironmentConnections` names the ones you
// may see.
//
// AN ARCHIVED DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT IS REFUSED with `deploy_environment_archived`: it takes no
// new runs, so nothing would ever use the binding.
//
// Takes a body of the `application/json` content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PUT /deploy-environments/{deploy_environment_id}/connections/{alias} (the `BindDeployEnvironmentConnection` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deployEnvironmentId DeployEnvironmentId, alias ConnectionAliasParameter, body BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionJSONRequestBody, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*BindDeployEnvironmentConnectionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.BindDeployEnvironmentConnection(ctx, deployEnvironmentId, alias, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseBindDeployEnvironmentConnectionResponse(rsp)
}

// PlaceOnDeployListWithBodyWithResponse Let a member or a team deploy to a deploy environment
//
// A PLACE ON THE ENVIRONMENT'S LIST OF WHO MAY DEPLOY. It grants
// `flows:deploy-environment.deploy` on this one deploy environment, and nothing else: "a
// place on the list grants Deploy here, and nothing else". Putting a flow live here takes
// that AND Deploy on the flow.
//
// NO ROLE IS PICKED. The list is a way of sharing that picks no role (the role register), so
// the grant carries the permission's id in place of a role's, as Keys' "Approve only" does
// (decision 0541).
//
// WITH A MEMBER OR A TEAM OF THIS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT — "Teams/members from Account (e.g.
// juniors → Development only)". Nobody by that id here, or no such team, is refused (409
// `recipient_not_found`).
//
// AN EMPTY LIST LETS NOBODY IN BY NAME, AND NOBODY IS LEFT WITH NO WAY TO DEPLOY. A role that
// holds the permission across the whole account environment reaches every deploy
// environment, and the built-in Administrator role holds every permission. The list is for
// everyone else.
//
// A BUSINESS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT ONLY, as the catalogue gives the act. In a personal one
// nobody holds it (403).
//
// PLACING THE SAME ONE AGAIN ANSWERS THE PLACE THEY HAVE. A holder has one grant per object
// (decision 0650), so `created` reads `false` and the id is the one they had: a retry after
// a timeout never makes a second place.
//
// NO END DATE. No page gives a place on this list one, so the request carries none; a place
// lasts until it is taken off.
//
// AN ARCHIVED DEPLOY ENVIRONMENT KEEPS ITS LIST, and it can be changed there: only a deploy
// into it is refused.
//
// KEEP THE PLACE'S ID: it is what takes this place off later. Reading the list is not
// declared here yet.
//
// NEVER AN AI AGENT'S TO DO, and never a raw key's: a place is a grant of access (403
// `ring_requires_person`).
//
// THE IDENTITY SERVICE KEEPS THE GRANT, AND WRITES IT ITSELF. When it cannot be reached, 503
// `identity_unavailable`: whether the place landed is not known, so send the same request
// again. It is the same act, and answers the place that is there.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /deploy-environments/{deploy_environment_id}/deployers (the `PlaceOnDeployList` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) PlaceOnDeployListWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deployEnvironmentId DeployEnvironmentId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*PlaceOnDeployListResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.PlaceOnDeployListWithBody(ctx, deployEnvironmentId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParsePlaceOnDeployListResponse(rsp)
}

