// ListDeploymentsWithResponse Which flows are live, and where
//
// What the Deployed tab shows: one row per flow per deploy environment it is live in, with
// the named version that is live there. Filter by deploy environment for the tab, or by flow
// to see everywhere one flow is live.
//
// ONLY WHAT YOU MAY SEE. Seeing where a flow is live is seeing the flow, so a flow in a
// project you hold no role on is absent, never masked.
//
// A CONNECTION THAT STOPPED WORKING IS ON THE ROW, in `connections_not_working`, read from the
// Integrations app when you ask. It goes when the connection works again or the flow's use of
// it ends. The row carries the connection's id: its name and its page are Integrations'
// `getConnection`, for a caller allowed to see it. Left out when Integrations could not be
// asked, because not known is not working.
//
// A CONNECTION SOMEBODY DISCONNECTED IS ON THE ROW TOO, in `connections_disconnected`, read
// the same way and left out on the same terms. It goes on Reconnect, or when the flow's use of
// it ends.
//
// AN ALIAS BOUND TO NOTHING IS ON THE ROW, in `aliases_bound_to_nothing`. A connection removed,
// or gone with its owner or its account environment, is unbound from its alias, so the row
// reads "Connection removed". This one is Flows' own fact, so Integrations is not asked and it
// is always there. It goes when a connection is bound to the alias again, or when the flow's
// use ends.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /deployments (the `ListDeployments` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListDeploymentsWithResponse(ctx context.Context, params *ListDeploymentsParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListDeploymentsResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListDeployments(ctx, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListDeploymentsResponse(rsp)
}

// ListFlowProjectsWithResponse The flow projects you may see
//
// ONLY WHAT YOU MAY SEE, AND NOTHING IS ASKED AT THE DOOR. Your own projects need no
// permission: what is only yours is seen without one (decision 0811, rule 4). Someone else's
// project is listed only where you hold `flows:project.read` on it, so that permission is
// asked of each row of the company's list (decision 0811, ruled for this list on 2026-10-16,
// ONPLO-2600). For a Company flows project it comes from a role on the account environment or
// on that project. A project you cannot reach is absent, never refused, so the list never
// tells you a project exists that you may not open. Holding a role on the whole account
// environment never reveals another person's Personal flows: those are theirs, and only a
// share on that project would, which is not built yet.
//
// IT STARTS EMPTY. Nothing is made for you: a person who has made no project and been
// given none sees an empty list, and makes a project before a first flow.
//
// YOUR PERSONAL FLOWS ARE EVERY PROJECT OF YOURS, wherever its folder is: in System → Flows,
// or made in a folder of your own (`createFlowProjectInOwnFolder`). THE COMPANY'S ARE EVERY
// PROJECT OF THE COMPANY'S you may read, in System → Flows or made in a company folder
// (`createCompanyFlowProjectInFolder`); each is asked `flows:project.read` alike. The order is
// not a promise; follow `next_cursor` to the end.
//
// ONE LIST PER OWNER-SCOPE, AND THE REQUEST SAYS WHICH. `owner_scope` is the split the menu
// shows — Personal flows are your own, Company flows are the company's — and each is a list
// of its own, as each is a page of its own. A personal account environment has only the
// first; asked for the company's there, the list is empty.
//
// IT ANSWERS IN THE ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT THIS CREDENTIAL ACTS IN and never beyond it: a flow
// project belongs to one environment, and switching environment switches the whole list.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /flow-projects (the `ListFlowProjects` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListFlowProjectsWithResponse(ctx context.Context, params *ListFlowProjectsParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListFlowProjectsResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListFlowProjects(ctx, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListFlowProjectsResponse(rsp)
}

// CreateFlowProjectWithBodyWithResponse Make a flow project
//
// "+ New project": WHERE YOU BUILD, one per client, team or domain. Nothing is made for you,
// so a first flow needs one made first.
//
// IT IS NAMED WHEN IT IS MADE. The screen prefills a name on a person's first one; an API
// has no screen, so the request says the name.
//
// `owner_scope` SAYS WHOSE IT IS, AND IT NEVER CHANGES: `personal` is your own, in Personal
// flows; `company` is the company's, in Company flows.
//
// ITS FOLDER GOES TO SYSTEM → FLOWS, in the storage of whoever owns it — yours, or the
// company's. A project IS a folder in the storage tree, and the id answered here is that
// folder's id in the Storage app. Choosing a folder of your own for it is another operation,
// `createFlowProjectInOwnFolder`.
//
// IT POINTS AT NO OBJECT, AND THAT IS A STATEMENT. A project sits inside nothing, so there
// is no container for "may I make one here" to be about, and the question is asked of the
// whole account environment.
//
// A COMPANY PROJECT IS MADE ONLY WHERE THERE IS A COMPANY SIDE: a business account
// environment, or Onplo's own team (decision 0663). Asked for in a personal one, it is
// refused with a 422 whose error points at `/body/owner_scope` with the code
// `company_project_in_a_personal_environment`, and nothing is made. Where the authorization
// service did not say which kind of account environment the request is in, a company
// project is answered `authorization_unavailable` — ask again — and never that refusal: not
// known is not personal. A project of your own is made in any kind.
//
// NOTHING IS SHARED BY MAKING IT. A new project is private to you, or follows the company's
// roles; sharing one is its own permission and is not here yet.
//
// AN AGENT OR A MACHINE MAKES A COMPANY PROJECT as a person does, in the company's storage.
// A project of somebody's own asked for by one is the 422 on `owner_scope` it always was:
// there is no person whose storage it would go in. THIS ANSWERED 501 FOR A COMPANY PROJECT
// until 2026-10-15, while Storage placed nothing for a caller that is not a person.
//
// A NAME ALREADY TAKEN IS REFUSED with `name_taken`, and nothing is made. A project is a
// folder, and two things in the same folder never carry the same name. Every project made
// here goes to System → Flows of its owner's storage, so two of your own cannot share a
// name, nor two of the company's; one of yours and one of the company's can — they sit in
// two storages. Capitals make no other name, and neither does another spelling of an
// accented letter; a name is kept as you typed it (ONPLO-1516). You give another.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /flow-projects (the `CreateFlowProject` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) CreateFlowProjectWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*CreateFlowProjectResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.CreateFlowProjectWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseCreateFlowProjectResponse(rsp)
}

