// CompleteFlowSaveWithResponse Complete a save
//
// THE FLOW IS SAVED FROM THIS MOMENT, and not before. What was there becomes an earlier
// version — every save is one, and none is lost.
//
// NOTHING IS HALF-WRITTEN. A save whose file has not arrived is refused (`save_incomplete`);
// one whose file is another size than the save declared is refused (`size_differs`), because
// the cap and the space were judged on the size declared.
//
// THAT THE FILE IS XML IS CHECKED AGAINST THE BYTES THEMSELVES, never only against what the
// upload claimed (`not_xml`). What is read is how the file opens: an XML declaration or a
// first element, after any comment. Whether the whole document is well-formed is not judged
// here; a web page is not XML.
//
// NOTHING HERE JUDGES WHAT THE FLOW SAYS. A half-built flow must be savable. What refuses a
// broken one is the check a deploy makes.
//
// TWO SAVES OF ONE FLOW DO NOT MERGE. The one completed later is the newest version, and the
// earlier one is in its history.
//
// ASKING TWICE IS SAFE. Completing a save that is already complete answers the same flow and
// changes nothing, so a completion whose answer was lost is simply asked again.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /flows/{flow_id}/saves/{save_id}/completion (the `CompleteFlowSave` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) CompleteFlowSaveWithResponse(ctx context.Context, flowId FlowId, saveId SaveId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*CompleteFlowSaveResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.CompleteFlowSave(ctx, flowId, saveId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseCompleteFlowSaveResponse(rsp)
}

// SimulateFlowWithBodyWithResponse Test a flow, or one step of it
//
// "TEST THIS STEP" AND SIMULATING A WHOLE FLOW ARE ONE WALK. It starts at the flow's trigger,
// from the example the trigger keeps, and goes step by step. Name a step in
// `stop_after_step_id` and it stops once that step is done; name none and it walks to the end.
//
// IT WALKS THE FLOW AS IT WAS LAST SAVED. A change still on its way in is not in the test.
//
// THE ANSWER IS THE WHOLE WALK: every stop in the order taken, what the step read and
// produced, which path it left by — so you watch a branch choose — every variable as it stands
// after it, and how long it took. A step that failed carries its error in plain words.
//
// NOTHING OUTSIDE ONPLO IS CHANGED, NOTHING IS DEPLOYED, AND NOTHING IS KEPT. The answer is
// the test: ask again to see it again.
//
// WHAT A TEST RUNS TODAY: the trigger's example, Transform data, and the branches. A step of
// any other kind — one that calls a connected system, asks an agent, waits for a person or
// for time — stops the walk there, and the answer says `unsupported_step` and names it. Each
// kind joins as the part of the app that runs it is built.
//
// A FLOW THAT NEVER ENDS IS CUT OFF. The walk takes at most `max_stop_count` stops, and an answer
// that hit the limit says so.
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION. Testing a flow is not designing it and not running it: Personal flows
// inside a company can be tested and cannot run.
//
// A `POST` because every call walks the flow again.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /flows/{flow_id}/simulations (the `SimulateFlow` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) SimulateFlowWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, flowId FlowId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*SimulateFlowResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.SimulateFlowWithBody(ctx, flowId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseSimulateFlowResponse(rsp)
}

